From bc4e15229be87e9310de9049c484dc7d98fb1641 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 04:51:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rework the interface as an instrument: cyan, sharp, and
 properly 3D
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The whole visual system is replaced. Nothing of the purple, rounded, glassy
version is left — no radii, no lavender, no soft cards.

The look. Corners are square or cut on a 45° from one token every panel,
button, sleeve and dialog shares; surfaces are separated by blue-tinted
hairlines rather than shadows; panels carry corner brackets and the window
sits on a faint 44px grid. Numbers, labels, section titles and sort headers
are monospace with wide tracking, because an instrument's readouts should line
up in columns and never reflow as the digits change. The light theme is a
drafting table — ice white, ink-blue rules, the same cyan doing the same job —
rather than the dark theme inverted.

Two accents, deliberately separate. --accent-rgb is the instrument's electric
cyan and never moves: it is the colour that points at things, and an interface
whose pointing colour changes every three minutes is one you cannot learn.
--art-rgb carries the current album's colour and is only used beside that
album's artwork — hero wash, sleeve glow, the far end of a gradient. A loud
cover tints its own corner; it cannot repaint the controls. Coverless albums
now generate their plate from the cool half of the wheel for the same reason.

Three dimensions, for real. js/gl.js is a small WebGL layer — shader helpers, a
column-major 4×4 stack whose every routine writes into a caller-owned array,
and geometry builders. backdrop.js uses it to draw a wireframe world: a ground
plane running to the horizon that scrolls and ripples with the bass, square
tunnel sections receding and swelling on a beat, an icosahedron turning above
the horizon, and a fullscreen pass for horizon glow, vignette and scanlines.
Geometry is uploaded once and animated in the vertex shader, so a frame is four
draw calls and no allocation.

The intro is a 3D scene now: five wireframe bars rise out of the grid in
sequence while the camera swings around and settles, with the wordmark
assembling over it. The flat SVG mark that ships in the HTML is the fallback.

Motion. motion.js gains scramble (text resolving out of noise, character by
character), tilt3d (pointer-tracked perspective on springs, used by every
card), countTo, a wipe option on enter, and settled. Entrances are gated on the
route actually having changed, so a repaint from the library updating
underneath no longer re-dissolves the heading you are reading.

Visualisers are drawn with the same pen: square bars with falling peak caps, an
oscilloscope over a ticked axis, spokes around an instrument dial, and a new
mesh mode — the last second of spectrum as a wireframe surface running to a
horizon.

Bugs found on the way:

- GLSL ES requires a name declared in both shader stages to carry the same
  precision, and the stages have different defaults. u_level was highp in the
  vertex shader and mediump in the fragment shader, so the program failed to
  link and the entire backdrop silently removed itself. Shared varyings are now
  qualified explicitly and no uniform is shared at all. The smoke test caught
  this, which is why it checks.
- An imported helper named `settled` was shadowed by a local boolean of the
  same name inside startIntro, so dismissing the intro threw and the overlay
  never left. Renamed the flag.
- Reordering the queue spliced state.queue directly, leaving the pre-shuffle
  order describing a sequence the queue no longer had; turning shuffle off
  afterwards restored the wrong one. Reordering is now player.moveInQueue.
- tilt3d measured its element inside pointermove, forcing a layout on every
  event; it measures once per hover instead.
- Exit animations gated node removal on anim.finished, which on a machine
  dropping frames can settle hundreds of milliseconds late. settled() races it
  against a timeout so the node always comes out.
- The backdrop now stops while an import is running: the worker's throughput is
  what someone is actually waiting on. That put import back to ~425 files/s.

Scroll frame time is unchanged at a median 16.7 ms with 28 live nodes.

