-From 0fc8ce704d6e6a98a70a4d3839525303c5121ca9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
-From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
-Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:19:18 +0000
-Subject: [PATCH] Add the intro, a 3D backdrop, four visualisers, and any audio
- format
-MIME-Version: 1.0
-Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
-Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit
-
-The interface now sits in a room rather than on a page, opens on a proper
-welcome, and draws what it is playing.
-
-Intro. index.html carries the opening sequence as markup so it is on screen at
-first paint instead of after the modules land; intro.js animates the mark, the
-wordmark and the meter while the library comes out of IndexedDB, and hands over
-when both are done. The first key, click or scroll skips it, a repeat visit gets
-it at roughly half length, and a CSS failsafe removes it after eight seconds if
-the scripts never arrive at all.
-
-Backdrop. backdrop.js is one WebGL canvas behind everything: an aurora of three
-metaballs in the current accent colour, and a few thousand points projected
-through a real perspective divide, looping toward the camera. Bass widens the
-field, level brightens it, a beat pushes a pulse through it. Two draw calls over
-static geometry at a capped pixel ratio, painted as a darker tint on light
-pages because light added to white is invisible, and it backs itself off to half
-rate and then out of the way on a device that cannot hold 60fps. No WebGL, and
-the CSS gradients that were always underneath are what you see.
-
-Analysis and visualisers. player.analysis() folds a 2048-point FFT into 64
-logarithmic bands with a tilt, asymmetric smoothing, falling peak caps and beat
-detection, once per frame and shared — so any number of canvases cost one
-reading. visualizer.js renders bars, an oscilloscope, a rotating radial ring or
-stacked ribbons; they run inside the now-playing artwork, as a hairline over the
-transport bar, and full-bleed on the new immersive stage (V), where the artwork
-tilts toward the pointer and leans into the beat and the chrome steps aside when
-the pointer goes still.
-
-Any audio type. The extension table now covers every audio container worth
-indexing, a File the OS calls audio/* is taken on that alone, and tags.js gained
-AIFF and Matroska/WebM readers plus magic-number sniffing for files whose suffix
-means nothing. Formats no browser decodes are still indexed, tagged and shown —
-their rows say "no decoder" — and everything else is attempted, with the
-decoder's own verdict marking the row if it fails. canPlayType is not consulted:
-it denies formats it goes on to play.
-
-Interface. Every chrome surface is glass over the backdrop, tokens are declared
-as channel triplets to make that one definition, cards lift on a real
-perspective, Home gained a header with generated vector artwork, and Settings
-gained the visualiser style and backdrop switches.
-
-Fixes and upkeep along the way: removing the playing track from the queue now
-closes the gap instead of jumping backwards, and playNext/enqueue/clearQueue
-keep the pre-shuffle order in step; a failed decode no longer retries forever
-under repeat-one; the disc-header test in the album view is no longer quadratic;
-the playhead unregisters its frame callback while paused, so an idle Sonora asks
-for no frames; the AIFF sample rate in the test generator was off by an octave.
-The tools take SONORA_CHROMIUM, wait for the intro, and cover the intro, the
-backdrop, both visualiser surfaces, mode switching and the undecodable path.
-
